INT. WIRELESS HUT - ATLANTIC COAST - NIGHT, 1941

A low shed of sandbags and corrugated iron. A single oil lamp swings from a hook. The sea batters the rocks outside. On a deal table, a wire recorder spins slowly beside a heavy radio set with a brass key.

CORPORAL EMMELINE ROSKILLY, twenties, in a greatcoat and a woollen cap, sits upright on a crate. A headset is clamped over one ear. Her pencil moves steadily over a message pad. She has not blinked in some time.

The radio crackles. Over it, a steady drone: enemy bombers, far off, growing nearer.

EMMELINE
(to herself)
Steady, girl.

She taps the key. Pauses. Taps again. The recorder spins, catching her.

EMMELINE (V.O.)
(recording)
Station Albatross to all vessels. Enemy aircraft sighted bearing two-seven-zero. Repeat, two-seven-zero. Take cover.

The lamp swings. A distant thump shakes dust from the rafters. She does not look up. She taps the key again.

EMMELINE (CONT'D)
(into mouthpiece)
Albatross to convoy. Confirm receipt.

Nothing. Static. The drone rises to a roar.

She reaches for the recorder and rewinds it a few inches. Plays it back. Her own voice again, steady.

EMMELINE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Take cover.

Another thump, closer. The lamp goes out. In the dark, her hands keep working the key, tap, tap, tap, as the walls shake around her.

EMMELINE (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Confirm receipt. Confirm receipt. Confirm...

A ragged voice breaks through the static, faint, hers alone in the room, played from the spinning spool.

EMMELINE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Take cover.

She keeps tapping.
